{ id:"d3-49", domain:3, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A production integration needs retries, a circuit breaker, and a fallback chain. The integration calls a downstream dependency shared with four other services, and product requires that a degraded-but-useful answer reach users instead of an error page. Which placement satisfies both stated constraints?",
  options:["All three patterns centralized in the orchestration layer behind one shared facade","Retries at the API call, a breaker per calling service tripping independently, fallbacks in the client","Retries at the API call, the breaker at the shared dependency's entry point, fallbacks in orchestration","Retries and fallbacks in each caller's application code, circuit breaking delegated to the provider"],
  correct:[2],
  explanation:"The shared dependency decides the breaker's home: at the shared entry point it trips on aggregate failure and protects all five callers at once, and orchestration is the only layer that can select an alternate path before the response is composed. Per-caller breakers (B) each need their own failure budget to trip and leave the fallback where no alternate can be chosen; A collapses distinct concerns into one layer; D delegates a client-side control to the failing side." },
{ id:"d3-50", domain:3, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"An EU deployment through a cloud-provider route must guarantee in-region model execution. Which configuration approach is defensible?",
  options:["Choose a documented regional endpoint or model route that guarantees regional routing, pin that configuration, and verify the deployed endpoint settings","Choose a cloud provider headquartered in Europe and use its global endpoint because corporate domicile establishes processing location","Use a global endpoint with a data-processing agreement because contractual residency removes the need for technical routing controls","Set the browser locale and SSO tenant region to Europe so downstream model execution inherits the user's geographic setting"],
  correct:[0],
  explanation:"Residency is controlled by the delivery route's documented endpoint or model configuration, such as an eligible regional route, and must be verified in the deployed integration. Provider domicile, contracts alone, browser locale, and identity-provider region do not determine where inference executes." },
{ id:"d3-51", domain:3, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"An internal Claude application uses Okta SSO and offers an optional calendar-write connector. What must occur before the application can create an event for a user?",
  options:["Treat successful Okta login as consent for every downstream connector scope available to the application","Validate SSO server-side, obtain explicit scoped connector consent and a delegated token","Place the user's role in the system prompt and let the model decide whether calendar write access is appropriate","Use one application-wide calendar service account and rely on provider audit logs to identify unauthorized writes"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"SSO authenticates the application user; it does not automatically grant downstream calendar authority. The application must validate the session server-side, obtain the user's scoped delegated consent, protect the resulting token, and authorize each event action. Prompted roles, broad service accounts, and audit-only controls do not establish that permission." }
