// Hard set
window.QUESTIONS = (window.QUESTIONS || []).concat([
{ id:"d3-52", domain:3, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A cross-system agent burns most of its budget on exploratory tool calls — listing projects, opening records one by one — before answering simple queries. The MCP server currently exposes everything as tools. What is the server-side fix?",
  options:["Add a meta-tool that returns the catalog of available tools with usage examples","Raise the agent's iteration limit so exploration can complete","Batch the exploratory calls into a single combined tool","Expose the browsable content as MCP resources, reserving tools for actions"],
  correct:[3],
  explanation:"MCP separates content from actions: resources make server content directly available as context, eliminating call-by-call exploration; tools remain for operations with effects. A meta-tool (A) and batching (C) optimize the wrong mechanism, and a higher iteration limit (B) funds the inefficiency instead of removing it." },
{ id:"d3-53", domain:3, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"An MCP server for a ticketing system needs credentials at runtime, and the configuration will be committed to the team repository. How should authentication be configured?",
  options:["Reference the secret via environment variable expansion, with the value injected per environment","Place the API key directly in the server's args so the configuration stays self-contained and portable","Have users paste the key into their first message of each session","Encode the key in the server URL, which the client treats as opaque"],
  correct:[0],
  explanation:"Committed configuration must carry references, not secrets: environment expansion keeps the credential out of the repository and lets each environment inject its own value. Args (B) and URLs (D) commit the secret in different costumes, and chat-pasted keys (C) put credentials into model context and logs." },
{ id:"d3-54", domain:3, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"An agent misroutes between search_tickets and search_archive, which differ only in that the archive holds tickets closed over a year ago. Selection accuracy matters more than latency here. What is the highest-leverage fix?",
  options:["Retire search_archive and grow date-range, status, and scope parameters on the main tool","Merge the two into one tool with an explicit combined description, splitting later if needed","Rewrite both descriptions with each tool's boundary, relationship, and explicit cutoff","Reorder the definitions so the more commonly used search tool is listed first"],
  correct:[2],
  explanation:"Semantically similar tools need descriptions that discriminate: purpose, boundary, and the explicit relationship between them. Merging (A, B) is a legitimate alternative design but changes the API rather than fixing selection, and definition order (D) is not a selection signal the model should be relying on." },
{ id:"d3-55", domain:3, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A refund flow requires fetch_account before issue_refund, but the model sometimes calls issue_refund directly with a guessed account state. Downstream, an unstructured reply is as bad as a wrong one. Which combination enforces correctness?",
  options:["State the ordering requirement prominently in both tool descriptions and the system prompt","Gate issue_refund on fetch_account's completion, forcing tool invocation where prose breaks the consumer","Validate server-side, rejecting any refund call that arrives without fresh account data attached","Lower the temperature so the documented call order is followed more reliably in practice"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"Prerequisites are enforced by construction: gate tool availability on the dependency and use forced invocation where a conversational reply is a failure. Descriptions (A) and temperature (D) make compliance likelier, not guaranteed; server-side rejection (C) is a needed backstop but turns every skipped prerequisite into a runtime error loop instead of preventing it." },
{ id:"d3-56", domain:3, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"One system serves two flows: agents answering live customer chats, and a nightly job classifying 1.8M archived conversations, with results needed before the next business day. How should the two flows map to API processing modes?",
  options:["Synchronous Messages API for the chats; the asynchronous batch mode for the nightly classification","Batch mode for both flows, with the chat interface polling until its results arrive","Synchronous for both, with the nightly job throttled to respect shared rate limits alongside the chats","Batch mode for chats to absorb traffic spikes; synchronous for the nightly job's predictability"],
  correct:[0],
  explanation:"Mode follows latency contract: interactive turns need synchronous responses; a latency-tolerant bulk job inside a processing window is what batch mode exists for, at better economics. Polling chats (B) and inverted assignment (D) violate the interactive contract, and all-synchronous (C) pays real-time prices for work nobody is waiting on." },
{ id:"d3-57", domain:3, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"Each assistant turn triggers a weather lookup, a calendar read, and a contacts search — independent calls the runtime currently executes across three sequential model round-trips. What is the correct configuration change?",
  options:["Cache the three lookup results so subsequent turns can skip most of the calls","Ask users to bundle their needs into a single request covering the three tools","Move the three lookups into one combined backend endpoint returning a merged payload parsed in one pass","Enable the loop to emit multiple tool calls per turn, the independent calls executing in parallel"],
  correct:[3],
  explanation:"Independent tool calls belong in one turn: parallel emission collapses three model round-trips into one, cutting the dominant latency source. Caching (A) misfits per-request-fresh data like calendars, user bundling (B) exports the problem, and a merged endpoint (C) couples unrelated services to work around a loop configuration." },
{ id:"d3-58", domain:3, type:"multi", select:2, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"In a multi-agent pipeline, a summarization subagent was observed calling the deployment tool, and a research subagent read HR records irrelevant to its task. Which TWO changes address the failure class?",
  options:["Add a coordinator instruction telling subagents to stay within their roles","Log each subagent tool call into a weekly access-review report","Assign each subagent only the tools its designated role requires","Route each subagent call through the coordinator for scoped oversight","Constrain each subagent with a role-scoped system prompt and scoped context"],
  correct:[2,4],
  explanation:"Out-of-role behavior is removed structurally: per-role tool restriction makes the wrong call impossible, and role-scoped prompts plus scoped context keep each agent inside its lane. Instructions (A) and logs (B) shape and observe rather than prevent, and full coordinator mediation (D) serializes the pipeline to compensate for missing scoping." },
{ id:"d3-59", domain:3, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A long-running code-migration agent is interrupted at 60% completion. On restart it must neither redo finished modules nor trust stale analysis of files that changed meanwhile. What does correct session resumption look like?",
  options:["Replay the full prior transcript turn by turn until the agent rebuilds its working state","Restart the migration from zero, reproducibility valued above any resumption gains","Resume from persisted state, re-analyze only the files changed meanwhile, inject the restored context","Resume from the persisted state as-is, prior analysis trusted, no re-scan of the changed tree"],
  correct:[2],
  explanation:"Resumption is persisted state plus targeted refresh: completed work carries over, and only the delta gets re-analyzed before context injection. Transcript replay (A) resurrects stale conclusions at full token cost, zero-restart (B) discards 60% of paid work, and as-is resumption (D) is the strongest trap — it trusts analysis the interruption made stale." },
{ id:"d3-60", domain:3, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A five-stage multi-agent pipeline processes thousands of records; infrastructure restarts are routine. Which persistence design makes the pipeline production-reliable?",
  options:["Keep pipeline state in the coordinator's context window for the session's duration","Checkpoint durable state after each completed unit, idempotent stages preventing repeat work","Rerun the full pipeline on any interruption, restart events treated as rare","Persist only final outputs, intermediate state re-derivable by rerunning the affected stages"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"Reliability under routine restarts means durable checkpoints at unit granularity plus idempotent stages — resumption becomes a no-op replay from the last checkpoint. Context windows (A) are not durable storage; full reruns (C) contradict the stated restart frequency; final-only persistence (D) makes every interruption forfeit all in-flight work." },
{ id:"d3-61", domain:3, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A competitive brief must cover six predefined market segments, each needing its own sources and context, under a tight same-day deadline. Reliability requires that one segment's failure not sink the brief. Which orchestration pattern fits?",
  options:["Parallel execution: six independent workers plus a synthesis pass, failing independently","Dynamic coordination: a coordinator discovering segments and delegating as it learns","A sequential pipeline: each segment building on the previous one's findings","A single shared context: one agent holding the six segments together"],
  correct:[0],
  explanation:"The subtasks are predefined and independent, the deadline rewards concurrency, and isolation contains per-segment failure — the parallel pattern's exact profile. Dynamic coordination (B) pays discovery overhead for segments already known; sequencing (C) creates false dependencies and a failure chain; one shared context (D) couples everything the requirements say to isolate." }
]);

